  "title": "git add -A in commitAll still runs a nested repository's filter",
  "file": "src/git/repos.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An agent plants a command in a nested repository's filter configuration and edits an ordinary parent file. When the factory commits the edit, it executes that command outside the agent's sandbox.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/adjudicate-539.ts\"",
    "output": "F1 protected status marker=false\nF1 commitAll returned commit=true marker=true\nF1 gitlink unchanged=true\ntrace: built-in: git add -A\ntrace: run_command: cd nested; unset GIT_COMMON_DIR GIT_GRAFT_FILE GIT_PREFIX GIT_SHALLOW_FILE GIT_WORK_TREE; GIT_DIR=.git git status --porcelain=2 -uno"
   },
   "evidence": "The temporary script imported HEAD's actual functions and created a recorded linked worktree containing a committed, unchanged gitlink and committed .gitmodules with ignore=none. After configuring the nested clean filter and changing its file's mtime, standalone protected status left the marker absent, but commitAll created it. HEAD repos.ts:450 calls `await worktreeGit([\"git\", \"add\", \"-A\"], { cwd });`; command.ts:143 injects protection only when `[\"status\", \"diff\"].includes(cmd[command] ?? \"\")`. The staging call is identical at base, so this is an incomplete delivery of the requested nested-repository protection rather than a newly introduced staging defect. The later commit replaces staging in commitAll, merge completion and implement evaluation with stageAll, which uses ls-files and update-index instead of add's internal status inspection, and adds the unchanged-gitlink regression case."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An agent sets up a nested repository with a clean filter in its own git config and leaves it unchanged except for a file timestamp. When the factory commits the agent's work, the daemon runs that filter outside the agent's sandbox, so it executes a command the agent chose. This happens with or without a committed .gitmodules entry that says ignore = none.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun $TMPDIR/f539/repro.ts <checkout>  (temp repo with a committed nested gitlink and .gitmodules ignore=none; nested .git/config has filter.attack.clean='touch marker; cat'; nested info/attributes has '* filter=attack'; nested file mtime changed; a parent file edited; then commitAll). Variant repro-noignore.ts drops the ignore=none line.",
    "output": "head: commitAll sha: a47b5385\u2026 marker: true; head without ignore=none: commitAll sha: a2db7547\u2026 marker: true; fix commit 22473cc9 (archived src): marker: false in both variants"
   },
   "evidence": "At head, commitAll (src/git/repos.ts:450) still runs `worktreeGit([\"git\", \"add\", \"-A\"])`. The new guard in src/git/command.ts:143 is `if ([\"status\", \"diff\"].includes(cmd[command] ?? \"\")) flags.push(\"--ignore-submodules=dirty\")`, so it never applies to `add`, and `add -A` runs its own status inside the populated nested repository. The head test misses this because it advances the nested HEAD before the second commitAll. The request requires that commitAll not run the nested filter and that ignore = none not re-enable it."
  }
 },
